{"name":"Antiquarian Brown","hex":"#946644","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"antiquarian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to antiquaries, or to antiquity."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A collector or student of, or expert in, antiquities or antiques."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antiquarian"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Antiquarian","blocks":[{"text":"An antiquarian or antiquary is an aficionado or student of antiquities or things of the past. More specifically, the term is used for those who study history with particular attention to ancient artefacts, archaeological and historic sites, or historic archives and manuscripts. The essence of antiquarianism is a focus on the empirical evidence of the past, and is encapsulated in the motto adopted by the 18th-century antiquary Sir Richard Colt Hoare, \"We speak from facts, not theory.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/1655_-_Frontispiece_of_Museum_Wormiani_Historia.jpg/330px-1655_-_Frontispiece_of_Museum_Wormiani_Historia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiquarian","wikidata":"Q5697103"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/500px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/1280px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/500px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Antiquaries\": portraits of 20 influential antiquaries and historians published in Crabb's Universal Historical Dictionary (1825). Featured are: Giraldus Cambrensis, John Leland, Guido Panciroli, John Stow, William Camden, Justus Lipsius, Joseph Justus Scaliger, Johannes Meursius, Hubert Goltzius, Henry Spelman, Charles Patin, Philipp Clüver, William Dugdale, Claudius Salmasius, Friedrich Spanheim, Johann Georg Graevius, Jakob Gronovius, Thomas Hearne, John Strype, and Elias Ashmole.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/William_Camden_Clarenceux.jpg/330px-William_Camden_Clarenceux.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/William_Camden_Clarenceux.jpg/330px-William_Camden_Clarenceux.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par